<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-product-coproduct-top-spaces" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Product and coproduct topology</title>

  <p> 	In this section we define product and coproduct of topological spaces. We begin with the following proposition.</p>
    <proposition xml:id="prop-product-of-top-spaces">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>I</m> be a nonempty indexing set, and <m>(X_i,\tau_i)</m> be topological spaces for each <m>i\in I</m>. We consider the product of sets, <m>X=\prod X_i</m> as in <xref ref="lem-existence-of-product-of-sets"/>. 	Following topologies on <m>X</m> are all equivalent.
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              The topology <m>\tau_1</m> on <m>X</m> generated by the following basis.
		          <me>\left\{\prod U_i:U_i\subseteq X_i\text{open, and all but finitely many } U_i=X_i\right\}</me>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              The <em>coarsest</em> topology <m>\tau_2</m> on <m>X</m> that make all projection maps <m>\pi_i\colon X\to X_i</m> continuous.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              (Universal property for the product topology). Let <m>\tau_3</m> denote the topology on <m>X</m> having the following universal property.
            </p>
            <p>
              For every topological space <m>Y</m> and every map <m>f\colon Y\to X</m>, <m>f</m> is continuous if and only if for each <m>i\in I</m>, <m>\pi_i\circ f\colon Y\to X_i</m> is continuous; see the following diagram.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-universal-property-of-product-of-top-spaces">
              <caption>Universal property of the product</caption>
            <image width="30%">              
            <latex-image>
              \begin{tikzcd}
              Y \arrow[d,"f",labels=left] \arrow[rd,"\pi_i\circ f"]\amp\\
              X \arrow[r,"\pi_i",labels=below]\amp X_i
              \end{tikzcd}
            </latex-image>
            </image>
            </figure>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
    </proposition>
    <proof>
      <p>
        <m>(1)\Leftrightarrow (2)</m>. Suppose that <m>U_i\subseteq X_i</m> is an open set. Then, <m>\pi_i^{-1}(U_i)</m> is the product of sets whose <m>j</m>-th component is <m>X_j</m> when <m>j\neq i</m> and the <m>i</m>-th component is <m>U_i</m>. Therefore, any topology on <m>X</m> which makes <m>\pi_i</m> continuous, we must have <m>\pi_i^{-1}((U_i))</m> open in that topology, in particular, <m>\pi_i^{-1}(U_i)\in\tau_2</m>. Furthermore, given a basis element <m>\prod U_i\in\tau_1</m> with <m>U_j=X_j</m> for any <m>j\not\in\{i_1,\ldots,i_n\}</m>, we can write it as <m>\bigcap_{k=1}^{n}\pi_{i_k}^{-1}(U_{i_k})\in\tau_2</m>. Hence, <m>\tau_1</m> and <m>\tau_2</m> are equivalent.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>(2)\Leftrightarrow (3)</m>. Suppose that <m>X</m> has topology <m>\tau_3</m>. Consider the identity map <m> {1}_X\colon X\to X</m>. Then, given an open subset <m>U_i\subseteq X_i</m>, <m>\pi_i^{-1}(U_i)=(\pi_i\circ {1}_X)^{-1}(U_i)</m> is open in <m>X</m> because <m> {1}_X</m> is continuous. Hence, <m>\tau_2\subseteq\tau_3</m>. Conversely, assume that <m>X</m> has given topology <m>\tau_2</m>, and let <m>f\colon Y\to X</m> be a continuous function. Given an open subset <m>U_i\subseteq X_i</m>, <m>(\pi_i\circ f)^{-1}(U_i)=f^{-1}\left(\pi_i^{-1}(U_i)\right)</m> is open in <m>Y</m>. Hence, <m>\pi_i\circ f</m> is continuous. Now assume that <m>\pi_i\circ f</m> is continuous for each <m>i\in I</m>. Let <m>\prod U_i</m> be basis element of <m>\tau_1</m>, i.e., <m>U_i=X_i</m> for all but finitely many <m>i</m>. Note that we have <m>f^{-1}\left(\prod U_i\right)=\bigcup_{i\in I}\left(\pi_i\circ f\right)^{-1}(U_i)</m>, and hence, <m>f^{-1}(\prod U_i)\in\tau_1=\tau_2</m>.

      </p>
    </proof>
    <definition xml:id="def-product-topology"><title>(Product of topological spaces)</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          The <em>product</em> of topological spaces <m>(X_\alpha,\tau_\alpha)</m> is the set <m>\prod X_\alpha</m> with the topology given by any one of the topologies in <xref ref="prop-product-of-top-spaces"/>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
    <p>
      We now consider the <q>coproduct topology</q>. We begin with the following proposition, which is analogues to <xref ref="prop-product-of-top-spaces"/>. We leave the proof of the proposition as an exercise.
    </p>
    <proposition xml:id="prop-coproduct-of-top-spaces">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>I</m> be a nonempty set, and let <m>(X_\alpha,\tau_\alpha)</m> be a topological space for each <m>\alpha\in I</m>. The following topologies on <m>\coprod X_\alpha</m> (see <xref ref="def-coproduct-of-sets"/>) are equivalent.
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              The topology <m>\tau_1</m> such that <m>U\subseteq\coprod X_\alpha</m> is open if and only if <m>U=\coprod U_\alpha</m> for some <m>U_\alpha\subseteq X_\alpha</m> open.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              The topology <m>\tau_2</m> such that it is the <em>finest topology</em> on <m>\coprod X_\alpha</m> such that inclusion maps <m>i_\alpha\colon X_\alpha\to\coprod X_\alpha</m> are continuous.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>(Universal property of the coproduct topology). 
              The topology <m>\tau_3</m> satisfying the following universal property.
            </p>
            <p>
              For every topological space <m>Z</m> and every function <m>f\colon\coprod X_\alpha\to Z</m>, <m>f</m> is continuous if and only if for each <m>\alpha\in I</m>, <m>f\circ i_\alpha\colon X_\alpha\to Z</m> is continuous, where <m>i_\alpha\colon X_\alpha\to\coprod X_\alpha</m> is natural inclusion.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-universal-property-of-coproduct">
              <caption>The universal property of the coproduct</caption>
              <image width="40%">
              <latex-image>
                
                \begin{tikzcd}
                  \amp \coprod X_\alpha \arrow[d,"f"] \\
                  X_\alpha \arrow[r,"f_\alpha",labels=below] \arrow[ru,"i_\alpha"] \amp Z                                 
                \end{tikzcd}
              </latex-image> 
                
              </image>
            </figure>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
    </proposition>
    <definition xml:id="def-coproduct-topology"><title>(Coproduct of topological spaces)</title>    
      <statement>
        <p>
          The <em>coproduct</em> of topological spaces <m>(X_\alpha,\tau_\alpha)</m> is the set <m>\coprod X_\alpha</m> with the topology given by any one of the topologies in <xref ref="prop-coproduct-of-top-spaces"/>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
</section>